    "knowledge_point_id": "916e6da9-a2d1-5bf9-9777-ce4ca4858b96",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Units of Acceleration",
    "problem": "If the speed of a surfer is measured in $\\text{m/s},$ what are the units of its acceleration?",
    "worked_solution": "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\text{m/s}$ and the time is measured in $\\text{s}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/4d419f405914437764e854b63682ddb6fa4c371730a2ec1cf4263b0afd2a847e.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{change in speed}}{\\text{change in time}},\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{units of speed}}{\\text{units of time}}=\\frac{\\text{m/s}}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are $\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$",
